{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 103, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Restoring a missing input-to-output residual path in conv-heavy cells gave large gains (#51,#52,#54,#55,#60), but swapping a conv or pool for a skip gave only small gains (#56-58)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing the residual skip on edge 3 with a conv usually hurt (#1-4,#7), but filling an empty or pooled edge 3 helped or was neutral (#8,#9)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "With edge 4 removed, the cell is shallow and has few paths to the output, so adding conv capacity on edge 3 helps a lot (#5,#6) instead of hurting (#1-4)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "When edge 4 is already absent, removing edge 5 cuts the main conv route into the output node, which was catastrophic (#83,#84,#90) compared with the milder losses elsewhere."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 1 absent, node 2 can only be reached through edge 0, so removing edge 0 disconnects the deep paths (#25,#30)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without the residual edge 3, deepening edge 0 makes optimization harder and was harmful (#96,#100), but with a skip on edge 3 the same edit helped (#91-99)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Adding depth on edge 5 in a cell with no residual edge 3 hurt (#20), while the same edit was positive in cells that have the skip (#11-19)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "If edge 5 is missing, filling edge 4 restores a parametric path into the output, giving large gains (#77) instead of marginal ones (#71-75)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 0 is absent, node 1 is dead, so edge 1 is the only input to node 2 and adding a conv there gave large gains (#68) compared with roughly neutral effects (#61-67)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "When the node-2 inputs are weak non-parametric pools, a conv on edge 5 adds needed learnable capacity (#14, and the analogous #69) compared with conv-fed node 2 (#11,#16-19)."}], "tok": 31899, "bad": 0, "probes": [[["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "none"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "none", "none"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["none", "none", "none", "skip_connect", "none", "none"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["none", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "avg_pool_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "none", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_1x1", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_1x1", "nor_conv_1x1"], [3, "nor_conv_3x3"]], [["nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "nor_conv_3x3", "skip_connect", "nor_conv_3x3", "s